The neighbourhood I(F₁) is contained in I(F₂) if and only if F₂ is contained in the ideal generated by F₁.

If the neighbourhoods are nested, F₁ belongs to I(F₁) because it is contained in the ideal it generates, so it belongs to I(F₂), which is the required containment. Conversely, if a finite set belongs to I(F₁), its generated ideal contains F₁, and the span law together with the hypothesis places F₂ inside that generated ideal.
